<p class="lede">This chapter goes from an empty folder to a running program, then a project with a test, covering the five ways you use <code>bayan</code> and your first compiler errors.</p>

<p>First chapter of the Bayan Book; read it in order: each builds on the one before, adds one idea, ends with a short quiz. For a faster pass, read <a href="tutorial.html">Your first Bayan program</a>, or <a href="reading.html">Reading Bayan code</a> for the unusual forms.</p>

<h2 id="install">What you need</h2>
<p>You need the <code>bayan</code> compiler on your <code>PATH</code>, and <code>zig</code>, which Bayan uses to turn its generated C into a native executable. <a href="install.html">Install Bayan</a> has the steps; then check your setup:</p>
<pre><code>bayan --version
bayan doctor</code></pre>
<p><code>bayan doctor</code> checks the C compiler, <code>zig</code>, <code>git</code> and the build cache, and says how to fix anything missing. The output here comes from macOS on Apple silicon.</p>

<h2 id="hello">Salam, world</h2>
<p>Make a folder with a file named <code>salam.byn</code>; Bayan sources end in <code>.byn</code>.</p>
<example name="book-01-hello"/>
<p>The whole program: no <code>main</code>, no import, no semicolon; statements can start a file and run from the top. Run it:</p>
<pre><code>bayan run salam.byn</code></pre>
<p><code>bayan run</code> compiles the file to a native executable and runs it. The result is cached: an unchanged file's second run starts at once. Everything after <code>--</code> goes to your program, not <code>bayan</code> (<a href="book-02-project-words.html">chapter 2</a> uses that).</p>

<h3 id="run-build-check">run, build and check</h3>
<table>
  <tr><th>Command</th><th>What it does</th></tr>
  <tr><td><code>bayan check salam.byn</code></td><td>Reports errors and warnings. Builds nothing. Use it while you edit.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>bayan run salam.byn</code></td><td>Compiles, then runs the program.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>bayan build salam.byn -o hello</code></td><td>Compiles to an executable you can copy and run on its own.</td></tr>
</table>
<p>All three take a file, a folder of <code>.byn</code> files or a project. Add <code>--mode fast</code> for optimized builds; the default is <code>safe</code>. Memory safety is not optional: bounds and region checks stay on in every mode.</p>

<h2 id="main">Statements and main</h2>
<p>Most programs start with top-level statements, because they are short; a larger one puts its work in functions and starts in <code>main</code>:</p>
<example name="book-01-main"/>
<p><code>string greeting(string name) =&gt; $"Salam, {name}!"</code> declares a function (<a href="book-04-functions.html">chapter 4</a> covers them). The return type comes first; <code>=&gt;</code> reads "evaluates to". <code>$"…{name}…"</code> is an interpolated string: braces hold an expression, whose value goes into the text.</p>
<p>One way in: with both <code>main</code> and top-level statements, the compiler stops:</p>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2013:</span> the program has two entry points
  <span class="gut">--&gt;</span> twoentry.byn:5:1

<span class="gut">  5 │</span> print("top level")
<span class="gut">    │</span> ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^ `main` is also declared on line 1

<span class="gut">✗ build failed: 1 error, 0 warnings</span>
<span class="gut">  explain: bayan explain BYN2013</span></pre>
<figcaption>Real output from the compiler (<code>bayan check twoentry.byn</code>). The path is shortened to <code>twoentry.byn</code>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<p>The message names the problem and points at the second entry; pick one. Top-level statements suit scripts and short examples; <code>main</code> suits anything with a few functions, and is where you read command-line arguments and report errors (<a href="book-02-project-words.html">chapter 2</a> does both).</p>

<h2 id="comments">Comments and newlines</h2>
<p>A comment starts with <code>//</code> and runs to the line's end. A <code>///</code> comment documents the item below it, in Markdown; there is no <code>/* */</code> form.</p>
<example name="book-01-comments"/>
<p>A newline ends a statement, so there are no semicolons. Write one and the compiler says to remove it:</p>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN1040:</span> `;` is an input form; a statement ends at the newline
  <span class="gut">--&gt;</span> semicolon.byn:1:23

<span class="gut">  1 │</span> print("Salam, world!");
<span class="gut">    │</span>                       ^ remove `;`
<span class="gut">    │</span>
<span class="gut">  help: remove the `;`</span>
<span class="gut">    │ 1 │</span> print("Salam, world!");
<span class="gut">    │   │</span><span class="add">                       -</span>

<span class="gut">✗ build failed: 1 error, 0 warnings</span>
<span class="gut">  explain: bayan explain BYN1040</span></pre>
<figcaption>Real output from the compiler (<code>bayan check semicolon.byn</code>). The path is shortened to <code>semicolon.byn</code>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<p>Every Bayan error has this shape, read top to bottom:</p>
<ol>
  <li>The first line has the code (<code>BYN1040</code>) and a sentence saying what is wrong.</li>
  <li>The arrow line gives file, line and column.</li>
  <li>The source line follows: <code>^</code> under the exact spot, a short label.</li>
  <li><code>help:</code> shows the fix; a line with <code>│</code> and a number is the corrected source, <code>+</code> added text, <code>-</code> removed.</li>
  <li>The last line names <code>bayan explain BYN1040</code>, a longer page about that code with an example and the usual fix.</li>
</ol>
<p>Two more first-hour errors; a misspelled name gets a suggestion:</p>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2001:</span> `prnt` is not defined
  <span class="gut">--&gt;</span> salam.byn:1:1

<span class="gut">  1 │</span> prnt("Salam, world!")
<span class="gut">    │</span> ^^^^
<span class="gut">    │</span>
<span class="gut">  help: did you mean `print`?</span>
<span class="gut">    │ 1 │</span> print("Salam, world!")
<span class="gut">    │   │</span><span class="add">   +</span>

<span class="gut">✗ build failed: 1 error, 0 warnings</span>
<span class="gut">  explain: bayan explain BYN2001</span></pre>
<figcaption>Real output from the compiler (<code>bayan check salam.byn</code>). The path is shortened to <code>salam.byn</code>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<p>A wrong-typed value is stopped where written. This label has no help line: nothing can be suggested, so the fix is yours.</p>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2202:</span> expected `i64`, found `string`
  <span class="gut">--&gt;</span> mismatch.byn:1:16

<span class="gut">  1 │</span> let age: i64 = "ten"
<span class="gut">    │</span>                ^^^^^

<span class="gut">✗ build failed: 1 error, 0 warnings</span>
<span class="gut">  explain: bayan explain BYN2202</span></pre>
<figcaption>Real output from the compiler (<code>bayan check mismatch.byn</code>). The path is shortened to <code>mismatch.byn</code>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<p>Read the message in full before changing anything; fix the first error first, as later ones can follow. The compiler shows the first 20 errors, and <code>--all</code> shows the rest.</p>

<h2 id="scripts">Scripts</h2>
<p>A script is a single file you run by name, starting with a line naming the program that runs it:</p>
<example name="book-01-script"/>
<p>Save it as <code>greet.byn</code>, <code>chmod +x greet.byn</code>, then <code>./greet.byn</code> or <code>bayan greet.byn</code>. A file starting with <code>#!</code> is a script that <code>bayan check</code>, <code>run</code>, <code>build</code> and <code>test</code> accept without a project. A <code>#:</code> line is a compiler directive; <code>bayan new greet --script</code> writes <code>#:io threaded</code>. When a script grows past one file, make it a project.</p>

<h2 id="projects">Projects</h2>
<p>A project is a folder with a <code>package.byn</code> file. Make one:</p>
<pre><code>bayan new demo</code></pre>
<pre><code>created app `local/demo` in `demo`

  cd demo
  bayan run
  bayan test</code></pre>
<p>It creates this layout (plus a <code>README.md</code>, an <code>.editorconfig</code> and <code>.gitignore</code>):</p>
<pre><code>demo/
  package.byn
  src/app/main.byn
  tests/app.test.byn
  testdata/
  docs/
  README.md</code></pre>
<p>Code goes in <code>src/</code>. A folder is a module: every <code>.byn</code> in <code>src/app/</code> belongs to module <code>app</code>. Tests go in <code>tests/</code>, as <code>.test.byn</code> files. <code>--lib</code> makes a library, <code>--script</code> a script; <code>bayan init</code> adds missing files to an existing folder.</p>
<p><code>package.byn</code> is data, not code: a struct literal checked against the <code>Package</code> type:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">Package {
    name: "local/demo",
    version: "0.1.0",
    edition: 2027,
    kind: .app,
    capabilities: [],
    deps: [],
    dist: Dist {
        targets: [.host],
        mode: .safe,
    },
}</code></pre>
<p><code>kind</code> says app (<code>.app</code>) or library (<code>.lib</code>). <code>capabilities</code> limits what the package may do. It is empty, so the package may not touch the file system, the network or anything outside itself; you will see why soon.</p>
<p>Inside, commands need no file name:</p>
<pre><code>cd demo
bayan run
bayan test</code></pre>
<p>The generated <code>src/app/main.byn</code> is ordinary Bayan: a function with a <code>///</code> comment, and a top-level statement last:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">/// The line this app prints.
string greeting() {
    return "Salam, world!"
}

print(greeting())</code></pre>

<h3 id="capabilities">Capabilities</h3>
<p>Put the word counter from <a href="book-02-project-words.html">chapter 2</a>, which reads a file, in a new project's <code>main.byn</code>: the first <code>bayan run</code> stops:</p>
<figure class="diag">
<pre>error BYN6004: `main` uses `fs`, which package `local/wc` does not declare in `capabilities`
  --&gt; src/app/main.byn:38:6

  38 │ void main(ReadOnlySpan&lt;string&gt; args) fails IoError {
     │      ^^^^ uses `fs`
     │
  help: add `.fs` to `capabilities`, or remove the use

✗ build failed: 1 error, 0 warnings
  explain: bayan explain BYN6004</pre>
<figcaption>Real output from the compiler, with the path shortened.</figcaption>
</figure>
<p>Reading a file is an effect. <code>capabilities</code> lists the effects the package may have; the compiler checks every function against that list. Add <code>.fs</code>, <code>capabilities: [.fs]</code>, and the program runs. This is how you tell what a dependency may do without reading its code. <a href="book-14-effects.html">Chapter 14</a> covers effects.</p>

<h3 id="tests">Tests</h3>
<p>The generated test file:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">test "greets the world" {
    assert(greeting() == "Salam, world!")
}</code></pre>
<p>A <code>test</code> block has a name and a body; <code>assert</code> checks a condition. <code>bayan test</code> compiles the tests and runs each in its own process:</p>
<pre><code>running 1 test in 1 module

◆ 1 passed · 0.48s</code></pre>
<p>Change the expected text to <code>"Salam, World!"</code> and run again. A failing assertion shows both sides:</p>
<pre><code>running 1 test in 1 module

  ✗ app: greets the world
      assert(greeting() == "Salam, World!")
             │          │
             │          false
             Salam, world!
      tests/app.test.byn:2

◆ 0 passed · ✗ 1 failed · 0 pending · 0.47s
  rerun the failure: bayan test --only "greets the world"</code></pre>
<p>The line under the failing <code>assert</code> shows each part: <code>greeting()</code> was <code>Salam, world!</code> and the comparison <code>false</code>. The last line gives the command that reruns only this test; <a href="book-12-testing.html">chapter 12</a> covers testing.</p>

<h2 id="other-commands">Other commands you will use</h2>
<table>
  <tr><th>Command</th><th>What it does</th></tr>
  <tr><td><code>bayan explain BYN2001</code></td><td>Prints a page about one diagnostic code.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>bayan format file.byn</code></td><td>Rewrites accepted-but-not-preferred forms (a trailing <code>;</code>, for example) to the preferred form. <code>--check</code> lists the files that would change and writes nothing.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>bayan clean</code></td><td>Removes the project's <code>out/</code> folder.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>bayan --help</code></td><td>Lists every command and option.</td></tr>
</table>
<p>The whole command line is in the <a href="ref-cli.html">reference</a>.</p>

<h2 id="quiz">Check your understanding</h2>
<p><code>q1.byn</code> runs with <code>bayan run q1.byn</code>.</p>
<example name="book-01-q1" output="no"/>
<details class="quiz">
  <summary>What does it print?</summary>
  <p>It prints three lines:</p>
<pre><code>start
item 2
end</code></pre>
  <p>The statements run from the top. <code>label(2)</code> calls a function declared above it, returning <code>item 2</code>; the declaration prints nothing.</p>
</details>
<example name="book-01-q2" output="no"/>
<details class="quiz">
  <summary>What does this program print?</summary>
  <p>It prints:</p>
<pre><code>Salam, Bayan!
done</code></pre>
  <p>The commented-out <code>print("hidden")</code> never runs, nor the third line's trailing comment. The <code>$</code> before the quote makes <code>{name}</code> an expression.</p>
</details>
<details class="quiz">
  <summary>Which command reports errors without compiling or running the program?</summary>
  <p><code>bayan check</code>. <code>bayan run</code> and <code>bayan build</code> compile first, so they find the errors too, then build an executable; <code>bayan explain</code> never reads your program, only prints a diagnostic page.</p>
</details>
<details class="quiz">
  <summary>You write <code>print("hi");</code>. What happens, and what do you do?</summary>
  <p>The compiler stops with <code>BYN1040</code>: <code>;</code> is not Bayan's syntax, because a newline ends a statement. The help line shows the fix, remove the <code>;</code>, and <code>bayan format</code> does it for you. It is an error, not a warning: the program does not build until it is gone.</p>
</details>
<details class="quiz">
  <summary>Why is <code>capabilities: []</code> in a new project, and what happens if a function in it reads a file?</summary>
  <p>An empty list means no effects. A function that reads a file has the file-system effect, so the compiler stops with <code>BYN6004</code> and tells you to add <code>.fs</code> to <code>capabilities</code> or remove the use. It is a package check, not a runtime failure: nothing runs with an undeclared effect.</p>
</details>

